# Connecting Dashboard to 3CX via SIP URI

A step-by-step guide for setting up your LiveKit Voice AI Dashboard so its agents can transfer calls directly to **3CX extensions, ring groups, and call queues** via SIP URI.

> **What this gives you:** when an inbound caller asks to speak to a person, the AI agent fires its `blind_transfer` (or `attended_transfer`) tool, which dials a SIP URI like `sip:391@your-3cx.example.com`. 3CX rings the matching extension/queue and bridges the caller through. With the dashboard's **AUTO Caller-ID** mode, the destination phone shows the **real caller's mobile number** so the 3CX user can see who's calling and call them back.

---

## Prerequisites

- A 3CX PBX (v18 or v20 verified) with admin access to **Voice & Chat → SIP Trunks**
- Your 3CX **SIP domain or public IP** (e.g. `your-3cx.example.com`)
- Knowledge of the **public IP** the dashboard sends SIP traffic from (the LiveKit SIP server's egress IP — see Part 3)
- One or more 3CX **extensions, ring groups, or queues** you want to be reachable
- Access to your LiveKit Dashboard with permission to edit Telephony → Outbound Trunks and Agent tools

---

## Part 1 — 3CX side

### Step 0 — Open the firewall ports

Before configuring the SIP trunk, make sure 3CX's firewall (and any cloud security group / network ACL in front of it) accepts inbound traffic from the dashboard's SIP egress IP on these ports:

| Port | Protocol | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| **5060** | **TCP** | SIP signaling (Step 4A configures the trunk for TCP — leave 5060 UDP closed unless you have another reason to open it) |
| **9000 – 10999** | **UDP** | RTP media (3CX's default RTP range — if you've customised it under 3CX → Settings → Network → Public, open that range instead) |
| 5061 | TCP | SIP/TLS signaling — **only if** you choose TLS instead of TCP (see FAQ at the bottom) |

Source the rules to the **dashboard's SIP egress IP only** (find it via Step 6 in Part 2 below) — not `0.0.0.0/0`. RTP is bidirectional once the call is established; most stateful firewalls allow the return traffic automatically.

> **Two layers to check.** Even if the OS-level firewall allows the traffic, 3CX has its own **Anti-Hacking** module that can independently block an IP after a few failed attempts. After opening the ports, also whitelist the dashboard's egress IP at **3CX → Security → Anti-Hacking → Whitelist** (Step 6 in Part 2 covers this).

### Step 1 — Create the SIP trunk

1. Log into the 3CX admin panel.
2. Go to **Voice & Chat → SIP Trunks**.
3. Click **+ Add SIP Trunk**.
4. On the *Choose your SIP Trunk VoIP Provider* screen, set:
   - **Country**: `Worldwide`
   - **Provider**: `Generic SIP Trunk (IP Based)`
5. Click **OK / Next**.

### Step 2 — General tab

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Name** | `Livekit Dashboard` |
| **Main Trunk Number** | `1234` (mandatory but unused for routing — any placeholder works) |
| **Default Route** | `End Call` (or a failsafe IVR/voicemail of your choice) |
| **Authentication ID** | *Leave blank* |
| **Authentication Password** | *Leave blank* |
| **Type of Authentication** | **`Do not require - IP based`** ← critical |
| **Registrar / Server** | The dashboard's SIP egress IP (see Part 3 below) |
| **Port** | `5060` |
| **Outbound Proxy** | *Leave blank* |

Click **Save**.

### Step 3 — DID Numbers tab

Add **one row per destination** the AI should be able to reach. Click **+ Add** for each.

| DID Number | Assigned To |
|---|---|
| `391` | User → e.g. Troy Paas (Ext 391) |
| `826` | Ring Group → e.g. Test RG (826) |
| `920` | Queue → e.g. test (920) |
| *(add more)* | Any extension, ring group, or queue you want reachable |

> The DID number must **exactly match** the user-part of the SIP URI the dashboard dials. So `INVITE sip:391@your-3cx.example.com` requires a DID row with `391`. Calls to numbers NOT in this list hit the trunk's Default Route. To add new destinations later, just add rows here — no dashboard change needed.

Click **Save**.

### Step 4 — Options tab

**4A — Configuration settings**

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Allow inbound calls | ✅ checked |
| Allow outbound calls | ✅ checked |
| **Transport Protocol** | **TCP** ← must be TCP, not UDP |
| SRTP Mode | Disabled |
| Simultaneous calls per trunk | `5` (raise as needed) |
| PBX delivers audio | ✅ checked |
| Disable Video | ✅ checked |
| **Include diversion header support** | ✅ **checked — required** |
| Allow carrier-side transfers (REFER) | ❌ unchecked |
| Support Reinvite | ❌ unchecked |
| Support Replaces | ❌ unchecked |

> **Why diversion header support matters**: the dashboard adds a `Diversion:` header to every outbound transfer INVITE so 3CX records the call as `creation_method=divert` in the CDR with the original inbound DID. Without this option enabled, 3CX may silently strip the header and the CDR loses transfer context.

**4B — Codec priority**

Remove every existing codec, then add in this exact order:

| Priority | Codec |
|---|---|
| 1 | **PCMU** |
| 2 | **PCMA** |

Remove `G.729`, `G.722`, and any other codec if present.

> The dashboard's `livekit-sip` server does **not** support G.729. If 3CX offers it first, the call will fail with `488 Not Acceptable Here`. PCMU + PCMA guarantees a successful negotiation.

Click **Save**.

### Step 5 — Verify on 3CX

Click **Check SIP Trunk** at the top of the trunk page → it should return green / OK. If it returns red, double-check Step 2's IP and Step 4's TCP transport before continuing.

